WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
Block two.

2
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.950
Calculus for machine learning.

3
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:08.450
On the page, this block is the section "Calculus for machine learning".

4
00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:14.875
This block has one destination: gradient descent, which is how every model in this course is trained.

5
00:00:14.875 --> 00:00:22.200
Everything in it is the minimum needed to understand that one procedure, and I will say where each piece is used as we go.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.075
A derivative tells you how fast a function's output changes when you change its input.

7
00:00:28.075 --> 00:00:32.700
Draw the tangent line at a point; the derivative is that line's slope.

8
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:41.025
If you have seen derivatives before, that is the whole idea, and the rest of this block is mostly about what happens with more than one input.

9
00:00:41.025 --> 00:00:46.725
Our example function for the block is f of x equals x minus three, all squared.

10
00:00:46.725 --> 00:00:50.425
A parabola with its lowest point at x equals three.

11
00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:53.950
Its derivative is two times x minus three.

12
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.400
Let us read some values.

13
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.175
At x equals one, the derivative is two times one minus three, which is minus four.

14
00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:05.650
Negative slope: going right takes you downhill.

15
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.900
At x equals three, it is two times zero, which is zero.

16
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:10.825
Flat.

17
00:01:10.825 --> 00:01:12.950
That is the bottom.

18
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:18.575
At x equals four point five, it is two times one point five, which is plus three.

19
00:01:18.575 --> 00:01:21.800
Positive slope: going right takes you uphill.

20
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:31.325
And the fact to carry forward: the derivative is zero where the function is momentarily flat, which happens at a peak, at a trough, or at a flat inflection point.

21
00:01:31.325 --> 00:01:36.450
That is how you find minima, and finding minima is what training a model is.

22
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:41.800
This function comes back in a few slides, as the thing gradient descent walks down.

23
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.700
A short table of standard derivatives, which are worth memorising rather than re-deriving each time.

24
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.675
A constant differentiates to zero.

25
00:01:51.675 --> 00:01:55.575
x to the n gives n times x to the n minus one.

26
00:01:55.575 --> 00:02:00.125
e to the x differentiates to itself, which is the defining property of e.

27
00:02:00.125 --> 00:02:03.625
The natural logarithm of x gives one over x.

28
00:02:03.625 --> 00:02:07.300
Sine gives cosine, and cosine gives minus sine.

29
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:12.900
I am deliberately not dwelling on those, because they are the standard list and you have met them.

30
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:18.500
But the last row is different, and it gets its own slide next, so let me just flag it.

31
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:28.250
It is the derivative of the sigmoid function, and the thing to notice is that it is written in terms of the sigmoid itself rather than in terms of e to the minus z.

32
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.900
That is not cosmetic, and the next slide says why.

33
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:38.125
The sigmoid is the S-shaped function: one divided by one plus e to the minus z.

34
00:02:38.125 --> 00:02:44.950
It squashes any number into the range between nought and one, and we meet it properly in Sessions seven and eight.

35
00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:50.400
Its derivative is remarkably tidy: sigma of z, times one minus sigma of z.

36
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.400
The derivative of the sigmoid, expressed using the sigmoid.

37
00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.525
Here is why that matters, and it is a point about computation rather than about mathematics.

38
00:03:00.525 --> 00:03:08.175
When a neural network processes an example, it computes sigma of z on the way forward, as part of producing its answer.

39
00:03:08.175 --> 00:03:14.875
Then, when backpropagation runs backwards to work out the gradients, it needs the derivative at that same point.

40
00:03:14.875 --> 00:03:26.000
Because the derivative is written in terms of sigma of z, and sigma of z has already been computed and is sitting in memory, the derivative costs one subtraction and one multiplication.

41
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.650
Nothing has to be recomputed.

42
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:38.025
Session nine evaluates this once per sigmoid unit per training example, which is an enormous number of times, so a cheap derivative is not a detail.

43
00:03:38.025 --> 00:03:40.100
Memorise the form.

44
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.625
You do not need the derivation.

45
00:03:43.225 --> 00:03:48.000
A picture for the fact from two slides ago, using functions you know well.

46
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.275
The solid curve is sine.

47
00:03:50.275 --> 00:03:53.525
The dashed curve is its derivative, cosine.

48
00:03:53.525 --> 00:04:00.100
Now look at every place where the sine curve reaches a peak or a trough, and look straight down at the cosine curve.

49
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.975
It crosses zero at exactly those points, every time.

50
00:04:03.975 --> 00:04:11.000
That is the whole statement, seen rather than said: where a function is momentarily flat, its derivative is zero.

51
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.275
And it is worth pausing on because it is how optimisation works.

52
00:04:15.275 --> 00:04:26.425
Finding the minimum of a function means finding where its derivative is zero, and when you cannot solve for that directly, which is almost always, you walk downhill until the slope flattens out.

53
00:04:26.425 --> 00:04:29.175
That walk is the next three slides.

54
00:04:29.775 --> 00:04:37.100
Most functions in machine learning have many inputs: a loss function has one input per weight, and there can be millions.

55
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.450
So we need derivatives with respect to each one.

56
00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:50.225
A partial derivative is exactly that, and the recipe is simple: pick one variable, treat every other variable as a constant, and differentiate as usual.

57
00:04:50.225 --> 00:04:57.000
Our example is f of x one, x two equals three x one squared, plus two x one x two.

58
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:02.425
For the partial derivative with respect to x one: treat x two as a constant.

59
00:05:02.425 --> 00:05:07.250
The first term, three x one squared, differentiates to six x one.

60
00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:15.925
The second term, two x one x two, has x one in it multiplied by the constant two x two, so it differentiates to two x two.

61
00:05:15.925 --> 00:05:19.175
Total: six x one plus two x two.

62
00:05:19.175 --> 00:05:23.250
Now with respect to x two, and this is where people trip.

63
00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.875
Treat x one as a constant.

64
00:05:25.875 --> 00:05:34.750
The first term, three x one squared, is now entirely constant, and the derivative of a constant is zero, so it contributes nothing at all.

65
00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:42.100
The second term is two x one times x two, with two x one constant, so it differentiates to two x one.

66
00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:47.725
So the two partial derivatives are six x one plus two x two, and two x one.

67
00:05:47.725 --> 00:05:55.650
If you only remember one thing: the term that vanishes is the one that does not contain the variable you are differentiating with respect to.

68
00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.250
Let us put numbers in, because that is what makes it stick.

69
00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:04.800
Evaluate at the point x one equals one, x two equals two.

70
00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:13.650
First partial derivative: six x one plus two x two, which is six times one plus two times two, which is six plus four, which is ten.

71
00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:18.875
Second partial derivative: two x one, which is two times one, which is two.

72
00:06:18.875 --> 00:06:22.500
So the gradient at that point is the vector ten, two.

73
00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:28.525
The gradient is simply all the partial derivatives collected into one vector, one per input.

74
00:06:28.525 --> 00:06:31.050
And now use Block one on it.

75
00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:34.075
This vector has a direction and a length.

76
00:06:34.075 --> 00:06:41.400
Its direction, mostly along the first axis, is the direction in which the function increases fastest from that point.

77
00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:48.000
Its length, which is the square root of a hundred and four, about ten point two, says how steep that increase is.

78
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.275
Two facts in one object: which way is uphill, and how steep.

79
00:06:52.275 --> 00:07:01.175
This is the number that PyTorch computes for you in Session two, when the same function is differentiated automatically and returns ten and two.

80
00:07:01.775 --> 00:07:08.125
Let me say the key property of the gradient as plainly as I can, because everything else depends on it.

81
00:07:08.125 --> 00:07:11.875
The gradient points in the direction of steepest increase.

82
00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:15.625
Uphill, as steeply as possible, from where you are standing.

83
00:07:15.625 --> 00:07:23.550
Which means that if you turn around and walk in the opposite direction, minus the gradient, you are heading downhill as fast as possible.

84
00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:27.050
And that is the entire idea of training a model.

85
00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:30.825
You have a loss function that measures how wrong your model is.

86
00:07:30.825 --> 00:07:32.750
You want it small.

87
00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:41.750
You compute the gradient of the loss with respect to the weights, which tells you which way is uphill, and you take a small step in exactly the opposite direction.

88
00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.750
Then you do it again.

89
00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:45.250
And again.

90
00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:55.750
That is gradient descent, it is the next two slides, and it is how every model in this course, from Session seven's perceptron to Session twelve's structure predictors, is trained.

91
00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.150
There is no second method.

92
00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:05.525
Here is the rule: the new x is the old x, minus a small number eta, times the derivative at the old x.

93
00:08:05.525 --> 00:08:10.400
Eta is called the learning rate, and it controls how big a step you take.

94
00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.450
Watch the minus sign, because it is the part people get wrong.

95
00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:18.825
The derivative points uphill, so we subtract it to go downhill.

96
00:08:18.825 --> 00:08:27.850
And when the derivative is negative, subtracting a negative number increases x, which is correct: a negative slope means downhill is to the right.

97
00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:34.425
Let us run it on our parabola, f of x equals x minus three squared, whose minimum is at three.

98
00:08:34.425 --> 00:08:38.775
Start at x equals zero, with eta equal to nought point three.

99
00:08:38.775 --> 00:08:41.650
From zero, we go to one point eight.

100
00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.325
Then two point five two zero.

101
00:08:44.325 --> 00:08:46.975
Then two point eight zero eight.

102
00:08:46.975 --> 00:08:49.200
Closing in on three.

103
00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.400
And here is the property I find elegant.

104
00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:58.775
The steps get smaller as we approach: one point eight, then nought point seven two, then nought point two nine.

105
00:08:58.775 --> 00:09:01.075
Nobody arranged that.

106
00:09:01.075 --> 00:09:08.475
They shrink because the derivative itself shrinks as you approach a flat minimum, and the step is proportional to the derivative.

107
00:09:08.475 --> 00:09:12.350
So you do not have to reduce eta by hand to land softly.

108
00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:14.775
The procedure does it for you.

109
00:09:14.775 --> 00:09:19.100
The next slide has the arithmetic in full, if you want to check it.

110
00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:24.325
The numbers, slowly, because this is worth being able to reproduce.

111
00:09:24.325 --> 00:09:27.600
The function is x minus three, all squared.

112
00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.125
Its derivative is two times x minus three.

113
00:09:31.125 --> 00:09:35.750
We start at x equals zero, with eta equal to nought point three.

114
00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:37.450
Step zero.

115
00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:39.325
x is zero.

116
00:09:39.325 --> 00:09:44.000
The derivative is two times zero minus three, which is minus six.

117
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:50.400
The update is zero minus nought point three times minus six, which is zero plus one point eight.

118
00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:53.125
So x becomes one point eight.

119
00:09:53.125 --> 00:09:54.600
Step one.

120
00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.900
x is one point eight.

121
00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:04.975
The derivative is two times one point eight minus three, which is two times minus one point two, which is minus two point four.

122
00:10:04.975 --> 00:10:10.775
The update gives one point eight plus nought point seven two, which is two point five two.

123
00:10:10.775 --> 00:10:12.075
Step two.

124
00:10:12.075 --> 00:10:17.900
The derivative is minus nought point nine six, and x becomes two point eight zero eight.

125
00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:19.375
Step three.

126
00:10:19.375 --> 00:10:25.200
The derivative is minus nought point three eight four, and x becomes two point nine two three.

127
00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:32.150
Read down the derivative column: six, then two point four, then nought point nine six, then nought point three eight four.

128
00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:37.250
Each one is smaller than the last, by the same factor, and eta never changed.

129
00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:43.075
From Session seven onwards you will watch this exact arithmetic happen inside a model.

130
00:10:43.675 --> 00:10:46.500
And here is the bridge out of this block.

131
00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:50.375
What we just did had one variable and one derivative.

132
00:10:50.375 --> 00:10:54.025
A real model has thousands or millions of weights.

133
00:10:54.025 --> 00:10:56.150
Does anything change?

134
00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:57.050
No.

135
00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:59.725
The update rule is identical.

136
00:10:59.725 --> 00:11:09.200
Instead of one derivative you have the gradient vector, with one partial derivative per weight, and instead of one subtraction you do one subtraction per weight.

137
00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.500
Same rule, applied many times in parallel.

138
00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:18.825
So there is genuinely nothing conceptually new between this toy parabola and training a neural network.

139
00:11:18.825 --> 00:11:23.475
If you understand these four steps, you understand the mechanics of training.

140
00:11:23.475 --> 00:11:31.975
What is missing is one thing: how do you get the gradient when the function is a deep composition, a function of a function of a function of your weights?

141
00:11:31.975 --> 00:11:36.000
Computing that by hand for a million weights is impossible.

142
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.350
That is the chain rule, and it is the next slide.

143
00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:41.700
The chain rule.

144
00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:53.750
For a composite function, y equals f of g of x, the derivative of y with respect to x is the derivative of f with respect to g, multiplied by the derivative of g with respect to x.

145
00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:59.800
In words: the derivative of a function of a function is the product of their individual derivatives.

146
00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:02.100
That looks unremarkable.

147
00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:06.675
It is the single most important fact in the second half of this course.

148
00:12:06.675 --> 00:12:08.425
Here is why.

149
00:12:08.425 --> 00:12:17.600
A neural network's output is a function of a function of a function of its weights: layer one feeds layer two, which feeds layer three, which feeds the loss.

150
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:23.275
A weight buried in the first layer affects the loss only through everything that comes after it.

151
00:12:23.275 --> 00:12:31.700
The chain rule says: to find how that weight affects the loss, multiply together the derivatives of every function it passes through on the way.

152
00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:34.025
That is backpropagation.

153
00:12:34.025 --> 00:12:44.500
Session nine does it properly, including the crucial efficiency trick that makes it cheap, but the whole algorithm is this one rule, applied systematically from the loss backwards.

154
00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:55.100
It is also, incidentally, where things go wrong: multiply enough numbers smaller than one together and you get nothing, which is the vanishing gradient problem of Session nine.

155
00:12:55.700 --> 00:13:00.225
Integration, briefly, because we use it in exactly one place.

156
00:13:00.225 --> 00:13:05.625
An integral is the area under a curve, and it is differentiation run backwards.

157
00:13:05.625 --> 00:13:18.025
If you differentiate a function and then integrate the result, you get back where you started, except for a constant, because the derivative of a constant is zero and so the integration cannot know what it was.

158
00:13:18.025 --> 00:13:25.150
On our example: integrating two times x minus three gives back x minus three squared, plus some constant.

159
00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.750
Where does this appear in the course?

160
00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:36.750
AUROC, in Session eight, which is the area under the ROC curve, a single number summarising a classifier's performance across all thresholds.

161
00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:40.250
That is an area under a curve, which is an integral.

162
00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:47.700
That is essentially the only place, which is why this slide is short and why the detail is in an optional box on the page.

